export const FIT_FOR = "## It fits well when\n\n**Your content is large, mixed and already yours.** Reports, contracts, policies, papers,\ntickets, transcripts, decks, spreadsheets. The value comes from the volume and the mess, because\nthat is what defeats keyword search and what nobody has time to read.\n\n**Provenance is not negotiable.** If an answer without a citation is useless to you - research,\nlegal, clinical, regulatory, anything audited - the citation trail is the product here rather\nthan a feature on top of it.\n\n**You are content heavy and people light.** A small team serving a large knowledge estate is\nthe shape this helps most. The reverse, a large team over a small estate, usually has cheaper\noptions.\n\n**You want one feature, not a programme.** Search that answers instead of listing. An assistant\ninside a product you already ship. Document triage. One surface, on real content, in production.\nThat is the pattern that reaches production; \"a proof of concept of everything\" is the pattern\nthat does not.\n\n**You are building an agent architecture and need the knowledge component.** It behaves as a\ncomponent. It speaks MCP, it is API-first, and it does not require being the platform.";
export const FIT_NOT_FOR = "## It fits badly when\n\n**You are researching retrieval itself.** If your work is inventing retrieval or ranking methods,\nyou want the pieces exposed, not integrated. Use a framework you can take apart - LangGraph,\nAutoGen or similar - and keep control of every step. A platform that makes good decisions for\nyou is the wrong tool when the decisions are your research.\n\n**You are acutely sensitive to latency or per-query cost at high volume.** Agentic retrieval\nplans, and planning means more model calls than a single-shot pipeline. A preprint comparing\nagentic and enhanced retrieval - Ferrazzi, Cvjeticanin, Piraccini and Giannuzzi, \"Is Agentic RAG\nworth it?\", submitted January 2026 and revised 20 April 2026, arxiv.org/html/2601.07711v2, so\ntreat it as early - measured the agentic setup using an average of 3.3 times the input tokens and\n1.9 times the output tokens, taking about 1.5 times longer, with cost up to 3.6 times higher, and\nconcluded that a well-optimised non-agentic pipeline can match or beat agentic performance while\nstaying more efficient. Read the body rather than the abstract: the abstract carries no figures.\nIf your questions are narrow and your volume is\nenormous, that finding probably applies to you. On this site, the reason most interactions feel\ninstant is that most of them never reach a generative call at all: browsing, searching and\nrecommending are retrieval only. That is a design choice available to you too.\n\n**Your questions are arithmetic over structured data.** \"What was revenue by region last\nquarter\" is a query, not a retrieval problem. Point it at the database. Where you have both -\nstructured telemetry alongside unstructured documents - the sensible shape is to let each system\ndo its own job and have the agent call the database as a tool rather than trying to read numbers\nout of prose.\n\n**Your content does not contain the answers.** This sounds obvious and it is the most common\nreal cause of disappointment. Ten thin documents cannot answer what a practitioner types. If the\nhonest answer is that the knowledge lives in people's heads, the first project is writing it\ndown, and no retrieval platform shortens that.\n\n**You need a specific deployment guarantee.** If a hard requirement governs where content may be\nprocessed or stored, settle it before anything else. Ask for the answer in writing and hold the\nevaluation until you have it. We would rather you did that than read a reassuring sentence on a\nweb page and discover the detail during security review.";
export const FIT_SUMMARY = "## The honest summary\n\nThis is a strong choice for putting trustworthy, cited answers over a large body of content you\nalready own, especially where being able to show your working matters. It is the wrong choice\nfor research into retrieval methods, for very high volume with very narrow questions, and for\nanything that is really a database query.\n\nIf you are not sure which side of that line you are on, ask this site. It has the material and\nit will decline rather than guess.";
